// Test: two dictionaries are equal when they hold the same keys with the same
// values — DM-22, decided 2026-08-19.
//
// Both Rust engines answered `#0` here and the browser engine `#1`, and `#0` was
// indefensible: every other collection compares by value, and a dictionary that
// never equals another cannot be tested, deduplicated or asserted on.
//
// Key ORDER is not part of it. Insertion order is preserved for walking, as in
// Python's dict, but two dictionaries built in a different order still hold the
// same thing.

p = #(x: 1, y: 2)
q = #(x: 1, y: 2)
r = #(a: 1, b: 2)
s = #(x: 1, y: 99)
o = #(y: 2, x: 1)          // same content, other insertion order
t = (1, 2)                // a POSITIONAL tuple is a different thing

>> "same keys and values: " (p == q) ¶
>> "different keys:       " (p == r) ¶
>> "same keys, one value: " (p == s) ¶
>> "order does not count: " (p == o) ¶
>> "positional vs dict:   " (t == p) ¶
>> "different length:     " (p == #(x: 1)) ¶

// `<>` is the negation, and has to agree
>> "negation agrees:      " ((p <> q) == #0) ¶

// Nested, and mixed value types
a = #(id: 1, tags: ["x", "y"], meta: #(ok: #1))
b = #(id: 1, tags: ["x", "y"], meta: #(ok: #1))
c = #(id: 1, tags: ["x", "z"], meta: #(ok: #1))
>> "nested equal:         " (a == b) ¶
>> "nested differs deep:  " (a == c) ¶

// Equality is the same relation as scalar equality, so 1 == 1.0 carries through
>> "Int/Float promotion:  " (#(k: 1) == #(k: 1.0)) ¶
